#!/bin/sh
# Refuse commits inside a Promptster assessment codespace.
#
# openspec codespaces-hosted-assessment §0.1a.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS, and it is not about tidiness. This mirror is a PUBLIC repo you
# do not have write access to. If you commit here, VS Code's Source Control panel
# offers to "Create Fork" — and that fork is a PUBLIC repository on YOUR OWN
# GitHub profile, containing your assessment work. GitHub does not let you change
# a fork's visibility afterwards. You can only delete it.
#
# So a habitual `git commit` costs you a permanently-public repo under your name
# until you notice and remove it. That is not a risk we are willing to leave to
# whether you happen to think about it.
#
# YOUR WORK IS CAPTURED WITHOUT COMMITTING. Promptster records the working tree
# and your session directly; `promptster done` submits it. Committing is not part
# of how you are assessed and never has been.
#
# If you have a real reason to commit, this is a hook and not a wall:
#   git commit --no-verify
cat >&2 <<'MSG'

  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  Commit blocked — and you do not need to commit.                   │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Your work is captured automatically. Run `promptster done` to submit.

  Committing here would prompt VS Code to fork this public repo onto your
  own GitHub profile. That fork is PUBLIC, it would contain your assessment
  work, and GitHub does not allow changing a fork's visibility later — it
  can only be deleted.

  Nothing about committing improves your submission. It is not assessed.

  If you genuinely need to:  git commit --no-verify

MSG
exit 1
